Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: User Not Appearing in Nested Groups
Problem: User is added to a nested group but doesn't appear in parent groups.

Solution:

# Check if the nested relationship is properly configured
ipa group-show parent-group --all

# Verify the child group exists and has the user
ipa group-show child-group --all

# Refresh the group cache
systemctl restart sssd
Issue 2: Permission Denied When Adding Members
Problem: Cannot add members to groups due to permission issues.

Solution:

# Ensure you're authenticated as admin
klist

# If not authenticated, re-authenticate
kinit admin

# Check if you have proper permissions
ipa permission-find --name="*group*"
Issue 3: Groups Not Showing in id Command
Problem: Groups don't appear when using the id command.

Solution:

# Clear SSSD cache
sss_cache -E

# Restart SSSD service
systemctl restart sssd

# Wait a moment and try again
sleep 5
id username
Verification and Testing Scripts
Script 1: Comprehensive Group Membership Check
Create a script to verify all group memberships:

#!/bin/bash
# group-membership-check.sh

echo "=== Group Membership Verification ==="
echo

users=("jsmith" "mjones" "bwilson" "slee" "testuser")

for user in "${users[@]}"; do
    echo "User: $user"
    echo "Direct groups: $(groups $user 2>/dev/null || echo 'User not found')"
    echo "Detailed info:"
    id $user 2>/dev/null || echo "User $user not found in system"
    echo "---"
done

echo "=== Group Structure Verification ==="
groups=("it-department" "hr-department" "finance-department" "all-managers")

for group in "${groups[@]}"; do
    echo "Group: $group"
    ipa group-show $group --all 2>/dev/null | grep -E "(Member users|Member groups|Indirect Member)"
    echo "---"
done
